{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[node_content]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This storage-only consumer must delegate load/store using node_path\n without resolving contents. Domain requires the callback, but invoking\n this guard would violate the no-content-read invariant of the test.\n Other graph fixtures exercise content resolution."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation is on an explicit function with a body, in fenced citation commentary, which is where the built-in allows it. The justification is substantive and matches the current source.\n\n- `impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[node_content]` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs` only panics with \"storage operations must not read graph contents\". The `Domain` trait requires the method, so it can't be left out.\n- `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` in the same impl delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage` using `node_path` and never call `node_content`.\n- The test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` checks that delegation. The `StoredReview` test also uses the graph without resolving contents. Executing the guard would break the test's no-content-read invariant, so zero coverage is expected.\n- The claim that other fixtures exercise content resolution holds. Real `node_content` implementations exist in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs` (`impl[Memory/Domain]`) and `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs` (`impl[Application/Domain]`), among others.\n\nThe citation carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference, so rule 2 (redundancy) doesn't apply.",
